markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Filtering

Imagine opening a music app and seeing a playlist labeled “Top 10 Songs Right Now.” Only ten songs appear even though thousands of songs exist on the platform.

<br>

<img src="https://images.code.org/87e2e5cfc91d0d45db89936e0818a5d1-image-1767631501751.png" alt="example list of top 10 songs" width="500px" style="border-radius:10px; float:right; margin: 0 10px 10px 20px;">

## Design the Playlist

You are designing the rule for the “Top 10 Songs Right Now” playlist.

**Before clicking, think about which rule you would use and what that rule might leave out.**

<div style="clear:both;"></div>

<div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2, 1fr);gap:16px;margin:24px 0;">

  <details style="background:#F5F7F8;border-radius:14px;padding:18px;box-shadow:2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
    <summary style="cursor:pointer;font-size:18px;font-weight:700;list-style:none;">
      🎧 Most streamed songs
    </summary>
    <div style="margin-top:12px;font-size:16px;line-height:1.5;">
      This rule keeps songs with the highest play counts.<br><br>
      <strong>What might get left out?</strong><br>
      Newer artists, local artists, or songs that are loved by smaller groups of listeners.
    </div>
  </details>

  <details style="background:#F5F7F8;border-radius:14px;padding:18px;box-shadow:2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
    <summary style="cursor:pointer;font-size:18px;font-weight:700;list-style:none;">
      🆕 Newest songs
    </summary>
    <div style="margin-top:12px;font-size:16px;line-height:1.5;">
      This rule keeps songs released most recently.<br><br>
      <strong>What might get left out?</strong><br>
      Older songs, classics, or songs that are still popular but not new.
    </div>
  </details>

  <details style="background:#F5F7F8;border-radius:14px;padding:18px;box-shadow:2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
    <summary style="cursor:pointer;font-size:18px;font-weight:700;list-style:none;">
      🌎 Trending near you
    </summary>
    <div style="margin-top:12px;font-size:16px;line-height:1.5;">
      This rule keeps songs that are popular in your location.<br><br>
      <strong>What might get left out?</strong><br>
      Songs trending in other communities, regions, or cultures.
    </div>
  </details>

  <details style="background:#F5F7F8;border-radius:14px;padding:18px;box-shadow:2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
    <summary style="cursor:pointer;font-size:18px;font-weight:700;list-style:none;">
      🌱 Emerging artists
    </summary>
    <div style="margin-top:12px;font-size:16px;line-height:1.5;">
      This rule keeps songs from artists with fewer total streams.<br><br>
      <strong>What might get left out?</strong><br>
      The most popular or familiar songs that many users may expect to see.
    </div>
  </details>

</div>

<div style="background-color:#B7E0E5;border-radius:30px;color:#000000;width:95%;padding:18px 24px;margin:24px 0;">

<p style="font-size:18px;line-height:1.5;margin:0;">
<strong>💡 Key Idea:</strong> A filter is not just a technical rule. It is a choice about what gets included, what gets hidden, and what users are most likely to see.
</p>

</div>

---

## What is a filter algorithm?

A **filter algorithm** is a rule a program uses to look through a list of data and keep only the items that meet certain conditions.

In JavaScript, filtering is often used with arrays of objects to narrow a large list into a smaller, more relevant one, like deciding which songs, messages, or results appear in a web app.

<br>

<div style="text-align:center;width:80%;margin:0 auto;">
  <p style="
    font-size:18px;
    background-color:#F5F7F8;
    padding:12px 18px;
    border-left:5px solid #8148B2;
    border-right:5px solid #8148B2;
    display:inline-block;
    line-height:1.5;
  ">
    Every filter starts as a <b>condition</b>.<br>
    Every condition is a <b>choice</b> that changes what is visible and what is hidden.
  </p>
</div>

---

## Quick Check

Think back to the playlist rule you chose.

<details>
<summary style="cursor:pointer;display:inline-block;background:#8148B2;color:white;padding:12px 20px;font-size:17px;font-weight:600;border-radius:999px;list-style:none;">
What condition did your filter use?
</summary>

<div style="margin-top:14px;background:#F5F7F8;border-radius:12px;padding:16px;font-size:16px;line-height:1.5;">
Your condition might have been based on stream count, release date, location, or artist popularity. The condition tells the program which songs to keep.
</div>
</details>

<br>

<details>
<summary style="cursor:pointer;display:inline-block;background:#8148B2;color:white;padding:12px 20px;font-size:17px;font-weight:600;border-radius:999px;list-style:none;">
What did your filter hide?
</summary>

<div style="margin-top:14px;background:#F5F7F8;border-radius:12px;padding:16px;font-size:16px;line-height:1.5;">
Every filter leaves something out. That is why students should think carefully about the rule they choose and what impact it has on the results.
</div>
</details>

---

## Next Up

